;;; game-lifecycle-multi-to-single-world.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · broken-examples
;;;
;;; A broken-example is kept ON PURPOSE (CLAUDE.md § Broken-examples teach the
;;; differences). This one teaches a whole MODEL change: the game/* LIFECYCLE
;;; verbs (game/frame, game/running?, game/state, game/step, game/stop) were
;;; documented against a MULTI-GAME-BY-ID model — every call took an integer
;;; `id` from `big-bang`, and many independent games ran side by side, each with
;;; its own frame counter and state cell. That model was inherited from Curator's
;;; multi-game RPC surface and was never wired in Motoi.
;;;
;;; Motoi has ONE world. `world/reset!` rebuilds it; entities are a flat list;
;;; the loop lifecycle is a single running flag and a single frame counter. So
;;; the game/* lifecycle verbs fold into world/* lifecycle verbs (chapter 72),
;;; and the `id` argument disappears — there is only ever one subject.
;;;
;;;   game/frame     id  →  world/frame               (chapter 72; already wired)
;;;   game/step      id  →  world/step                (chapter 72; already wired)
;;;   game/running?  id  →  world/running?            (chapter 72)
;;;   game/stop      id  →  world/stop                (chapter 72)
;;;   game/state     id  →  world/state               (chapter 72)
;;;   (no game/start)    →  world/start               (chapter 72; new pair to world/stop)
;;;
;;; IMPORTANT: the fold is NOT a pure textual swap — the single-world verbs have
;;; DIFFERENT return contracts than the multi-game records claimed. Those
;;; differences are the real lesson. The verbatim 5-verb multi-game chapter is
;;; archived at scratch/28-game.lifecycle-records.original.txt. Chapter 28 is now
;;; the combinatorial game-theory library only (game/grundy, game/nim-sum,
;;; game/surreal-*, etc.) — 22 verbs, all wired.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "broken-example — game/* lifecycle folded into world/* (single-world model)")

(broken-example :name "game/step"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "model-fold-multi-game-to-single-world-and-return-contract"
  :std-target "Motoi world/*"
  :std-rejected ("game/* lifecycle (unwired multi-game-by-id surface)")
  :status "folded"
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme/reference/broken-examples/game-lifecycle-multi-to-single-world.slatl"

  ;; ─── THE BROKEN CODE (unbound symbol; wrong model) ───
  :broken-code "(let ((id (big-bang 'x))) (game/step id) (game/step id) (game/frame id))"
  :broken-output "error: unbound symbol: game/step (and game/frame)"
  :runs "no — the game/* lifecycle verbs were never wired; the world is the only game"

  ;; ─── WHY IT IS BROKEN ───
  :why
"Motoi runs ONE world. There is no registry of games keyed by id, so a verb that
takes an `id` from `big-bang` and scopes a private frame counter to it has
nothing to bind to. The multi-game-by-id lifecycle (game/frame, game/running?,
game/state, game/step, game/stop) was a paper surface inherited from a
multi-game host. In Motoi the loop lifecycle is the world's: one frame counter
(world/frame), one running flag (world/running?), one step (world/step). The
`id` argument is not renamed — it is REMOVED, because there is only ever one
subject to talk about."

  ;; ─── THE FIX ───
  :fix-code "(begin (world/reset!) (world/step) (world/step) (world/frame))"
  :fix-output "2 — the world's monotone frame counter after two steps"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note
"Drop the `id`. `game/step id` → `world/step`; `game/frame id` → `world/frame`.
`world/reset!` gives the fresh-instance semantics that `big-bang` used to. The
frame counter starts at 0, increments by 1 per world/step, and is preserved
across world/stop — the same invariants the game/frame record proved, now on the
single world."

  ;; ─── THE LESSON ───
  :lesson
"When a lifecycle was documented per-instance but the engine has exactly one
instance, Motoi drops the instance handle rather than faking a registry. Port by
reading the target's REAL contract, not by assuming the multi-game docs were right:
  · game/step returned a status symbol ('ok/'done/'error/'nan) scoped to an id;
    world/step advances the one world and its status is the world's — you witness
    progress by reading world/frame before and after, not by threading an id.
  · game/running? / game/stop took an id and returned per-instance liveness;
    world/running? and world/stop act on the single world. world/stop preserves
    the frame counter (world/frame survives), and world/start — a NEW pair with
    no game/* ancestor — resumes a stopped world (idempotent when already live).
  · game/state returned an opaque per-id state cell or 'nan on unknown id;
    world/state returns a small status alist ((:frame N) (:running bool)
    (:entities count)) for the one world — there is no unknown-id case, so there
    is no 'nan sentinel."

  ;; ─── THE SAME IDEA IN THREE OTHER LIBRARIES ───
  :three-other-libs (
    (:lib "Motoi world/*"
     :status "the wired target — one world, no instance handle"
     :code "(begin (world/reset!) (world/step) (world/running?) (world/state))"
     :note "world/frame + world/step already documented; world/running?/stop/start/state complete the loop lifecycle. No id anywhere.")
    (:lib "LÖVE (Lua)"
     :status "one implicit game loop; love.update(dt) / love.timer / love.event.quit()"
     :code "function love.update(dt) frame = frame + 1 end   love.event.quit()"
     :note "Like world/*: a single ambient loop, not a table of game objects. quit() halts the one loop, mirroring world/stop.")
    (:lib "Pico-8"
     :status "one cart, one _update()/_draw() loop; no game-instance handles"
     :code "function _update() t += 1 end   stop()"
     :note "Same single-world shape: the console is the one game; there is no id to pass. stop() ends the one loop like world/stop.")
  )

  :motoi-standard
"Target: `world/frame` / `world/step` / `world/running?` / `world/stop` /
`world/start` / `world/state` (chapter 72) as the single-world loop lifecycle.
The game/* lifecycle surface dropped from the reference 2026-07-25; the verbatim
5-verb multi-game chapter archived at
scratch/28-game.lifecycle-records.original.txt. Chapter 28 is now the
combinatorial game-theory library only (22 verbs, all wired).")
